blocks.body:
If an equation has an integer solution, then it has a solution modulo every $m$ (see [[congruences]]). Contrapositively, if for some $m$ no choice of residues satisfies the congruence, there is no integer solution. The art is to choose $m$ so that the terms of the equation take few values.

Powers are the main source of such moduli:

- squares modulo $4$ are $\{0, 1\}$, so $y^2 \equiv 2, 3 \pmod 4$ has no solution; squares modulo $8$ are $\{0, 1, 4\}$;
- cubes modulo $9$ are $\{0, 1, 8\}$, and cubes modulo $7$ are $\{0, 1, 6\}$;
- fourth powers modulo $16$ are $\{0, 1\}$;
- fifth powers modulo $11$ are $\{0, 1, 10\}$.

The pattern behind the last three: $k$-th powers take few values modulo $m$ when $k$ shares a large factor with $\varphi(m)$, by [[fermat-euler|Euler's theorem]]. For example $a^5 \equiv \pm 1 \pmod{11}$ for $11 \nmid a$, because $(a^5)^2 = a^{10} \equiv 1$.

Some typical consequences. A sum of two squares is never $\equiv 3 \pmod 4$. A sum of three squares is never $\equiv 7 \pmod 8$, since three elements of $\{0, 1, 4\}$ cannot add to $7$ modulo $8$. A sum of three cubes is never $\equiv 4$ or $5 \pmod 9$, because sums of three elements of $\{0, 1, -1\}$ lie in $\{-3, \dots, 3\}$.

Two warnings about what this move can and cannot do. It proves non-existence only; finding that a congruence is solvable for one $m$ proves nothing. And even solvability modulo every $m$ is not enough: Selmer showed in 1951 that $3x^3 + 4y^3 + 5z^3 = 0$ has nonzero solutions modulo every integer $m$ (and over the reals), but no solution in integers other than $(0,0,0)$.

